{"question": "What Are Slot Snaps?", "answer": "Slot Snaps record the total number of snaps taken in the slot between the outermost receiver and the offensive line. This NFL advanced stat is a key part of NFL player stats for evaluating how a player is used in inside passing routes. Slot alignments can help create mismatches against linebackers or safeties, open space for quick throws, and diversify a team’s offensive attack. Within NFL team stats, slot usage patterns can show how a team’s passing game is structured, how often they target inside routes, and which players are most involved in them."}
{"question": "What Is Slot Rate?", "answer": "Slot Rate measures the percentage of a player’s total snaps taken from the slot position. This NFL player stat is an important NFL advanced stat for understanding a player’s role in the passing game and how often they operate inside rather than on the perimeter or in the backfield. High slot rates often point to specialized route-running skills, quickness in tight spaces, and the ability to exploit defensive coverage gaps. From an NFL team stats perspective, this percentage can help illustrate offensive philosophy, identify go-to players in short-yardage or third-down situations, and reveal how different positions are deployed in various game plans."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter Rate?", "answer": "Perimeter Rate measures the percentage of a player's dropback snaps taken on the boundary rather than inside, and it is the companion to Slot Rate: one counts the snaps a receiver spends in the slot, the other the snaps he spends outside. Outside alignments are where press coverage and a defence's top cornerback live, so a high perimeter rate describes a receiver an offence is willing to leave in one-on-one situations without scheme help, while a low one points to a player moved inside to find easier releases and softer coverage. Perimeter Rate sums both sides of the formation, so it pairs naturally with Left and Right Alignment Rate when the question is not how often a receiver lines up outside but which boundary he lines up on. For a wide receiver, slot and perimeter account for essentially every dropback snap. Tight ends and backs also take snaps in-line and in the backfield, which belong to neither, so for those positions the two rates will not add up to the player's whole workload."}
{"question": "What Is Left Alignment Rate?", "answer": "Left Alignment Rate measures the percentage of a player’s dropback snaps taken lined up on the left side of the formation, outside the slot. This NFL advanced stat completes the Slot Rate picture in NFL player stats, showing how often a receiver or tight end works from the left perimeter rather than the right side or the slot. A high left alignment rate can point to a favorable matchup a coaching staff wants to feature, a specific split tendency, or a receiver who has earned a consistent role on one side of the field. Left Alignment Rate, Slot Rate and Right Alignment Rate do not add up to a player’s whole workload: for a pure outside receiver they account for nearly all of it, but backfield, in-line and move-TE snaps belong to none of the three, so a tight end or running back’s totals will fall well short of 100%."}
{"question": "What Is Right Alignment Rate?", "answer": "Right Alignment Rate measures the percentage of a player’s dropback snaps taken lined up on the right side of the formation, outside the slot. This NFL advanced stat completes the Slot Rate picture in NFL player stats, showing how often a receiver or tight end works from the right perimeter rather than the left side or the slot. A high right alignment rate can point to a favorable matchup a coaching staff wants to feature, a specific split tendency, or a receiver who has earned a consistent role on one side of the field. Left Alignment Rate, Slot Rate and Right Alignment Rate do not add up to a player’s whole workload: for a pure outside receiver they account for nearly all of it, but backfield, in-line and move-TE snaps belong to none of the three, so a tight end or running back’s totals will fall well short of 100%."}
{"question": "What Are In-Line TE Snaps?", "answer": "In-Line TE Snaps track the total number of snaps where a tight end lines up attached to the formation, next to the offensive tackle, rather than detached in the slot, out wide, or in the backfield. This NFL advanced stat is a core part of NFL player stats for evaluating a tight end's traditional, in-line role, where blocking responsibilities and shorter, contested routes are more common than the space-creating routes a flexed tight end runs. In NFL team stats, In-Line TE Snaps can reveal how run-heavy or traditional an offense's formations are, and which tight ends are trusted as extra blockers versus receiving specialists."}
{"question": "What Is In-Line TE Percentage?", "answer": "In-Line TE % measures the percentage of a team's dropback snaps where a tight end lines up in-line, attached to the formation next to the tackle, rather than detached from the line. This NFL player stat is a staple among NFL advanced stats for analyzing a tight end's role in the passing game. A high In-Line TE % often means the player is used primarily as a blocker and short-area target, while a low mark points to a more flex/receiving-oriented usage."}
{"question": "What Are Move TE Snaps?", "answer": "Move TE Snaps -- also called flex TE snaps -- track the total number of snaps where a tight end lines up detached from the line of scrimmage, in the slot, split out wide, or in the backfield, rather than attached in-line next to the tackle. This NFL advanced stat is a core part of NFL player stats for identifying tight ends used more like a big slot receiver than a traditional blocker. In NFL team stats, Move TE Snaps can show which offenses lean on flexed tight ends to create mismatches against linebackers and safeties."}
{"question": "What Is Move TE Percentage?", "answer": "Move TE % measures the percentage of a team's dropback snaps where a tight end lines up detached from the line -- in the slot, out wide, or in the backfield -- rather than in-line. This NFL player stat is a staple among NFL advanced stats for analyzing a tight end's role in the passing game. A high Move TE % often means the player is deployed more like a receiver, running routes from space instead of blocking on the line."}
{"question": "What Are First Downs?", "answer": "First Downs records the total number of times a player’s action results in a new set of downs. This NFL advanced stat is a core part of NFL player stats for evaluating a player’s ability to sustain drives and keep an offense moving. By converting key plays into first downs, players extend possessions, give their team more scoring opportunities, and maintain momentum. In NFL team stats, first down totals help identify efficient offenses, strong rushing attacks, or passing games that consistently move the chains. Tracking this stat over time also highlights individual consistency and a player’s value in high-leverage situations."}
{"question": "What Is First Read Target Share?", "answer": "First Read Target Share measures the percentage of a player’s targets that occur as the quarterback’s first read; this only reflects games the player appeared in. This NFL player stat is an important NFL advanced stat because it reflects how much trust and priority a player has within the offensive scheme. A high first read target share indicates a primary receiving role, while a lower one might suggest secondary or situational usage. In NFL team stats, this metric helps reveal passing game hierarchies and how quickly quarterbacks look to their top playmakers. It’s also useful for identifying receivers who command immediate attention from defenses and drive offensive production."}
{"question": "What Is RB Opportunity Share?", "answer": "RB Opportunity Share measures the percentage of a team’s total rushing attempts and passing targets given to a player. This NFL advanced stat is a critical part of NFL player stats for understanding a player’s workload and role in the offense. A high RB opportunity share indicates that the player is a focal point, handling a significant portion of the team’s offensive touches. In NFL team stats, RB opportunity share can highlight offenses built around a single star or reveal balanced distribution among multiple players. Tracking this stat over time shows trends in player utilization and how coaching decisions impact production."}
{"question": "What Is Motion Rate?", "answer": "Motion Rate measures the percentage of a team's pass plays on which a player goes in motion before or at the snap — any pre-snap motion counts, not only players still moving when the ball is snapped. It divides by the same team passing plays that Route Participation uses, so the two NFL advanced stats read on the same scale: a high Motion Rate marks a player his offense deliberately moves to create leverage, force coverage tells, or win free releases, while a low rate points to a stationary role. Motion data begins with the 2023 season."}
{"question": "What Is Route Participation?", "answer": "Route Participation measures the percentage of a player’s snaps where they run a route. This NFL player stat is a valuable NFL advanced stat for understanding passing game involvement on plays when the player is on the field. A high route participation rate suggests a primary role in the aerial attack, while a low rate may indicate blocking duties or limited involvement in certain formations. In NFL team stats, this figure helps reveal how often a player is used as a legitimate receiving option, and it can expose under-the-radar contributors who make the most of their opportunities."}
{"question": "What Are Routes Run?", "answer": "Routes Run records the total number of routes a player runs during passing plays. This NFL advanced stat is a core part of NFL player stats for gauging a player’s involvement in the passing game. The more routes a player runs, the more chances they have to earn targets and contribute to the offense. In NFL team stats, routes run data can reveal offensive tendencies, personnel usage, and differences in scheme. It’s also valuable for comparing workload between players in similar positions and understanding how consistently they are placed in situations to make plays."}
{"question": "What Is True Target Share?", "answer": "True Target Share measures the percentage of a team's total pass attempts directed at a specific player. This NFL player stat is an important NFL advanced stat for showing a player's share of the passing volume. A high true target share indicates a player who commands a large portion of the team's passing game, while a low true target share might suggest a secondary role. In NFL team stats, true target share can reveal offensive focal points, passing distribution patterns, and how defenses adjust to take away preferred options."}
{"question": "What Is Target Share?", "answer": "Target Share measures the percentage of a team’s pass attempts directed at a player. This NFL player stat is a key NFL advanced stat for determining a player’s priority in the passing game. A high target share typically signals a go-to receiver or tight end who drives offensive production, while a lower share might indicate a complementary role. In NFL team stats, target share helps analyze offensive distribution, quarterback preferences, and how defensive game plans shift when covering top weapons. Over time, it’s a reliable indicator of sustained involvement."}
{"question": "What Are Targets Per Route Run?", "answer": "Targets per Route Run measures the average number of targets a player earns per route run. This NFL player stat is a widely used NFL advanced stat for evaluating efficiency in drawing quarterback attention. A high rate means the player is targeted often when running routes, reflecting strong separation, route-running skill, or offensive design. In NFL team stats, this metric helps identify which players are most productive per opportunity and whether their role is being maximized. It’s also valuable for spotting breakout candidates who are highly efficient despite limited routes."}
{"question": "What Is Snap Rate?", "answer": "Snap Rate, also called Snap Share, measures the percentage of a team's total offensive snaps that a player is on the field for. This NFL advanced stat is a core usage metric, showing how much coaches rely on a player in the game plan. A high snap rate signals a near-every-down role, while a lower rate points to situational usage or rotation. For NFL team stats, snap rate reveals depth chart structure and how heavily teams lean on their starters versus backups across a season."}
