{"question": "What Is CPOE?", "answer": "Completion Percentage Over Expectation (CPOE) measures the difference, in percentage points, between a quarterback's actual completion percentage and the completion percentage expected given each throw's difficulty. The expectation comes from a model that accounts for air yards, pass location, pressure, field position, and game situation on every attempt. This NFL player stat is one of the most stable NFL advanced stats for isolating quarterback accuracy from the difficulty of the throws they attempt. A positive CPOE means a passer completes more throws than expected; a negative CPOE means they complete fewer. In NFL team stats, CPOE is often paired with EPA per play to separate quarterback skill from scheme and supporting cast."}
{"question": "What Is Expected Completion Percentage?", "answer": "Expected Completion Percentage estimates the completion rate a quarterback should post given the difficulty of every pass they attempt. A model assigns each throw a completion probability based on air yards, pass location, pressure, field position, and game situation, and those probabilities are averaged across all attempts. This NFL player stat is a context-setting NFL advanced stat: a low expected completion percentage signals an aggressive, high-difficulty throw profile, while a high one signals a short, low-risk passing diet. In NFL team stats, it pairs naturally with actual completion percentage and CPOE to show whether a passer's accuracy numbers come from skill or from easy throws."}
