{"question": "What Is RB Rush Share?", "answer": "RB Rush Share measures the percentage of a team's running-back-position carries that go to this player -- distinct from two other share stats on the site. Rush Share divides by every carry the TEAM has at any position (RB, QB scrambles/sneaks, WR jet sweeps, and so on), so it can understate a bell-cow back's true lead over the other backs on his roster when the offense also leans on designed QB runs. RB Rush Share instead divides only by carries from players listed at running back, isolating how a team splits its backfield touches among its own RB room. Touch Share and RB Opportunity Share measure something different again -- they combine carries with targets (total touches/opportunities), not carries alone. Use RB Rush Share to settle who is winning the committee at running back, Rush Share to see how heavily an offense leans on its RBs as a group versus other positions, and Touch Share/Opportunity Share to weigh receiving work alongside rushing work."}
{"question": "What Is Stacked Box Rate?", "answer": "Stacked Box Rate is the percentage of a running back's carries that come against a stacked box -- a stacked box is eight or more defenders in the box at the snap. It completes the Stacked Box family alongside Stacked Box Yards (total production against loaded fronts) and Stacked Box Yards Per Carry (efficiency against them) by adding the workload-context piece: how often a back actually faces that resistance in the first place, rather than just how well he performs when he does."}
{"question": "What Are Air Yards?", "answer": "Air Yards records the total distance the football travels in the air from the line of scrimmage to the point of catch or incompletion. This NFL player stat is a usage-depth NFL advanced stat for intended downfield involvement. In NFL team stats, it shows how far QBs are targeting a receiver."}
{"question": "What Is Air Yards Percentage?", "answer": "Air Yards Percentage (Air %) measures the percentage of a team’s total air yards accounted for by a player. This NFL player stat is a market-share NFL advanced stat for deep passing volume. Because air yards can be negative on behind-the-line throws (screens, swings), a player’s single-game share can occasionally read above 100% when teammates’ negative-depth targets pull the team total down."}
{"question": "What Are Air Yards per Route Run?", "answer": "Air Yards per Route Run measures the average air yards generated for every route a player runs. This NFL player stat is an opportunity-depth NFL advanced stat for deep threat involvement. In NFL team stats, it shows per-route vertical usage."}
{"question": "What Are Intended Air Yards?", "answer": "Intended air yards measures the total air yards on all targets thrown to a player."}
{"question": "What Is Catchable Air Yards?", "answer": "Catchable Air Yards quantify how many of a player's total air yards came on accurate, on-target throws within the receiver's catch radius. This metric separates true opportunity quality from total passing volume by isolating passes that could realistically be caught. It helps identify players with actionable opportunity, providing insight into expected fantasy points and efficiency models like Catchable Air Yard Rate (CAY%). \"Catchable\" is a judgment about accuracy, not distance: air yards can be negative (a screen or swing pass thrown behind the line of scrimmage still counts), and a negative-depth throw can still be catchable."}
{"question": "What Is Catchable Air Yards Percentage?", "answer": "Catchable air yards percentage measures the proportion of air yards that come on catchable targets: Catchable Air Yards / Air Yards. Because \"catchable\" is a judgment about accuracy rather than distance, a single badly overthrown deep ball can carry more air yards than every catchable throw in the same game combined — so this rate is only meaningful over a real sample of targets, which is why the page defaults to a 2-targets-per-game minimum. What is Non-Catchable Air Yards (Prayer Yards)? Non-Catchable Air Yards represent the portion of total air yards from uncatchable throws — overthrows, underthrows, or passes out of bounds. This highlights inefficiency and wasted opportunity in a passing offense. A high NCAY indicates poor quarterback accuracy or target quality, while a low NCAY reflects efficient and accurate passing. Non-Catchable Air Yards = Air Yards - Catchable Air Yards, so the two always add back up to a player's total Air Yards."}
{"question": "What Is Non-Catchable Air Yards Percentage?", "answer": "Non-catchable air yards percentage measures the proportion of air yards on uncatchable targets: (Air Yards - Catchable Air Yards) / Air Yards — the complement of Catchable Air Yards Rate, so the two always sum to 100%. The same accuracy-not-distance caveat applies: one uncatchable deep shot can dominate a small sample, which is why the page defaults to a 2-targets-per-game minimum."}
{"question": "What Are Unrealized Air Yards?", "answer": "Unrealized Air Yards records the total air yards on incomplete passes to a player. This NFL player stat is a missed-opportunity NFL advanced stat for unrealized production. In NFL team stats, it reflects potential left on the field."}
{"question": "What Is Average Depth of Target?", "answer": "Average Depth of Target (aDOT) measures the average distance downfield where a player is targeted. This NFL player stat is a usage-depth NFL advanced stat for role profiling. In NFL team stats, it shows whether a player is used short, intermediate, or deep."}
{"question": "What Is Average Cushion?", "answer": "Average Cushion measures the average distance in yards between the receiver and the nearest defender at the snap. This NFL player stat is a pre-route space NFL advanced stat for alignment tendencies. In NFL team stats, it shows defensive respect or coverage strategy."}
{"question": "What Is Receiver Target Separation?", "answer": "Receiver Target Separation measures the average distance between the receiver and the nearest defender at the moment of target. This NFL player stat is a space-creation NFL advanced stat for route-running effectiveness. In NFL team stats, it shows ability to get open."}
{"question": "What Is Target Separation?", "answer": "Target Separation measures the average distance between a player and the nearest defender at the moment of target. This NFL player stat is a separation NFL advanced stat for space creation. In NFL team stats, it reflects ability to get open."}
{"question": "What Is Catchable Target Rate?", "answer": "Catchable Target Rate measures the percentage of a player’s targets deemed catchable. This NFL player stat is a target-quality NFL advanced stat for accuracy. In NFL team stats, it reflects QB ball placement and pass quality. What are Catchable Targets? Catchable Targets represent the total number of passes thrown to a player that are considered catchable. This NFL advanced stat helps evaluate a quarterback’s accuracy and receiver reliability. In team metrics, it highlights pass quality and offensive efficiency."}
{"question": "What Is Contested Catch Rate?", "answer": "Contested Catch Rate measures the percentage of contested targets successfully caught. This NFL player stat is a tight-coverage NFL advanced stat for win rate in traffic. In NFL team stats, it shows ability to secure difficult catches."}
{"question": "What Is True Catch Rate?", "answer": "True catch rate measures the percentage of catchable targets that are caught."}
{"question": "What Are Drops?", "answer": "Drops records the total number of catchable passes a player fails to secure. This NFL player stat is an opportunity-loss NFL advanced stat for missed catches. In NFL team stats, it reflects lost production."}
{"question": "What Is Drop Percentage?", "answer": "Drop % (Rank) measures the percentage of catchable targets dropped by a player, often ranked against peers. This NFL player stat is a reliability NFL advanced stat for hands quality. In NFL team stats, it shows drop rate comparison."}
{"question": "What Is Target Accuracy?", "answer": "Target Accuracy measures the percentage of a player’s targets that are both catchable and well-placed. This NFL player stat is a precision NFL advanced stat for QB-target synergy. In NFL team stats, it reflects pass quality."}
{"question": "What Are Yards per Route Run?", "answer": "Yards per Route Run measures the average receiving yards gained for each route run. This NFL player stat is an efficiency NFL advanced stat for total route production. In NFL team stats, it shows yards gained per route."}
{"question": "What Are Yards per Target?", "answer": "Yards per Target measures the average receiving yards gained for each target. This NFL player stat is an efficiency NFL advanced stat for per-target production. In NFL team stats, it shows return on opportunities."}
{"question": "What Are Yards After Catch?", "answer": "Yards After Catch records the total yards a player gains after securing a reception. This NFL player stat is a post-catch NFL advanced stat for playmaking ability. In NFL team stats, it shows YAC production."}
{"question": "What Is Receiver Air Conversion Ratio?", "answer": "Receiver Air Conversion Ratio (RACR) measures how efficiently a player converts air yards into receiving yards. This NFL player stat is a conversion NFL advanced stat for deep-ball productivity. In NFL team stats, it shows ability to turn targets into gains."}
{"question": "What Are Yards Per Team Pass Attempt?", "answer": "Yards per team pass attempt measures a receiver's yards relative to total team passing attempts."}
{"question": "What Is Dominator Rating?", "answer": "Dominator Rating combines a player’s share of team receiving yards and touchdowns into a single percentage. This NFL player stat is a market-share NFL advanced stat for total production impact. In NFL team stats, it shows offensive dominance."}
{"question": "What Are Routes vs Man?", "answer": "Routes vs Man records the total routes run by a player against man coverage. This NFL player stat is a usage NFL advanced stat for man-coverage exposure. In NFL team stats, it shows how often they face man defense."}
{"question": "What Are Routes vs Zone?", "answer": "Routes vs Zone records the total routes run by a player against zone coverage. This NFL player stat is a usage NFL advanced stat for zone-coverage exposure. In NFL team stats, it shows how often they face zone schemes."}
{"question": "What Is Target Rate vs Man?", "answer": "Target Rate vs Man measures the percentage of a player’s routes against man coverage that result in a target. This NFL player stat is an involvement NFL advanced stat for man-matchups. In NFL team stats, it shows usage rate in man defense."}
{"question": "What Is Target Rate vs Zone?", "answer": "Target Rate vs Zone measures the percentage of a player’s routes against zone coverage that result in a target. This NFL player stat is an involvement NFL advanced stat for zone-matchups. In NFL team stats, it shows usage rate in zone defense."}
{"question": "What Are Targets per Route Run vs. Man?", "answer": "Targets per Route Run vs Man measures the average number of targets a player earns per route run against man coverage. This NFL player stat is an efficiency NFL advanced stat for man defense matchups. In NFL team stats, it reflects involvement in man situations."}
{"question": "What Are Targets per Route Run vs. Zone?", "answer": "Targets per Route Run vs Zone calculates how many targets a receiver gets per route when facing zone coverage. This shows which players excel at becoming available targets against zone schemes."}
{"question": "What Are Yards per Target vs. Man?", "answer": "Yards per Target vs Man measures the average receiving yards per target against man coverage. This NFL player stat is a man-efficiency NFL advanced stat for production versus tight defense. In NFL team stats, it shows matchup efficiency."}
{"question": "What Are Yards per Target vs. Zone?", "answer": "Yards per Target vs Zone measures the average receiving yards per target against zone coverage. This NFL player stat is a zone-efficiency NFL advanced stat for production versus soft zones. In NFL team stats, it shows matchup efficiency."}
{"question": "What Is YPRR vs Man?", "answer": "YPRR vs Man measures yards per route run specifically against man coverage. This NFL player stat is a man-efficiency NFL advanced stat for per-route production. In NFL team stats, it shows matchup impact."}
{"question": "What Is YPRR vs Zone?", "answer": "YPRR vs Zone measures yards per route run specifically against zone coverage. This NFL player stat is a zone-efficiency NFL advanced stat for per-route production. In NFL team stats, it shows how effectively a receiver converts routes into yardage when facing zone schemes."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 0 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 0 Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against Cover 0 — all-out man press with no deep safety. High Cover 0 Rate indicates a receiver who draws pressure packages frequently."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 1 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 1 Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against Cover 1 — man coverage with a single high safety. It is one of the most common shells receivers face."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 2 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 2 Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against Cover 2 zone. Receivers who thrive vs. Cover 2 often exploit the middle windows between the underneath defenders and the safeties."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 3 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 3 Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against Cover 3 — a three-deep zone with one high safety and corner thirds. Routes attacking the seams and curl-flat windows are most effective here."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 4 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 4 Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against Cover 4 (Quarters). Receivers facing heavy Cover 4 often see soft underneath coverage and need to win on shorter routes."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 6 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 6 Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against Cover 6 — a hybrid coverage combining Cover 2 to one side and Cover 4 to the other. Routes targeting boundary alignments often exploit the coverage asymmetry."}
{"question": "What Is Single High Rate?", "answer": "Single High Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against single-high safety looks (Cover 1 or Cover 3). Receivers who excel vs. single-high often have the speed or route-running to beat isolated coverage."}
{"question": "What Is Two High Rate?", "answer": "Two High Rate measures the percentage of a receiver's routes run against two-high safety shells (Cover 2, Cover 4, or Cover 6). Two-high coverages limit big plays and often invite underneath routes."}
{"question": "What Is Target Premium?", "answer": "Target Premium measures how far a player's targets per route run exceed the 15% league baseline, in percentage points. This NFL player stat is a usage NFL advanced stat for target-earning ability. In NFL team stats, it shows which players command targets whenever they run a route."}
{"question": "What Is Target Quality Rating?", "answer": "Target Quality Rating combines accuracy, catchability, and depth metrics to score overall target quality. This NFL player stat is a target-caliber NFL advanced stat for opportunity evaluation. In NFL team stats, it shows the strength of passing opportunities."}
{"question": "What Is Target Separation vs Man?", "answer": "Target Separation vs Man measures the average separation created on targets against man coverage. This NFL player stat is a man-separation NFL advanced stat for beating tight coverage. In NFL team stats, it shows one-on-one win ability."}
{"question": "What Is Target Separation vs Zone?", "answer": "Target Separation vs Zone measures the average separation created on targets against zone coverage. This NFL player stat is a zone-separation NFL advanced stat for finding soft spots. In NFL team stats, it shows zone-beating skill."}
{"question": "What Is Weighted Opportunity Rating?", "answer": "Weighted Opportunity Rating (WOPR) combines target share and air yards share into a weighted metric for opportunity quality. This NFL player stat is a volume-adjusted NFL advanced stat for role importance. In NFL team stats, it shows weighted usage value."}
{"question": "What Is Win Rate vs Man?", "answer": "Win Rate vs Man measures the percentage of routes where a receiver beats his defender in man coverage."}
{"question": "What Is Win Rate vs Zone?", "answer": "Win Rate vs Zone measures the percentage of routes where a receiver beats his defender in zone coverage."}
{"question": "What Are 100+ Yard Receiving Games?", "answer": "100+ Yard Receiving Games measure how often a player hits the 100-yard receiving mark in a single contest, a key benchmark for dominance and consistency."}
{"question": "What Are Explosive Receptions?", "answer": "Explosive Receptions counts every reception of 20+ yards, capturing a receiver's big-play ability the same way Explosive Runs tracks it for running backs and quarterbacks. It fills the receiving side of that positional gap, highlighting players who consistently turn catches into chunk gains."}
{"question": "What Is Explosive Catch Rate?", "answer": "Explosive Catch Rate -- also called explosive reception rate -- measures the percentage of a receiver's catches that go for 20+ yards, showing how often their receptions turn into chunk plays rather than short gains. Only the 20-yard threshold is tracked (no 10/30/40/50-yard variants)."}
{"question": "What Is Yards After Catch per Reception?", "answer": "Yards After Catch per Reception divides a player's total Yards After Catch by their receptions, giving the average post-catch yardage on every catch. This is the overall per-reception rate; man/zone and alignment splits of YAC live in the site's tools rather than as separate pages."}
{"question": "What Is QB Rating per Target?", "answer": "QB Rating per Target -- also called passer rating when targeted -- applies the standard NFL passer rating formula (completions, yards, touchdowns, and interceptions) to only the throws aimed at one receiver. Unlike Target Quality Rating, which scores how accurate and catchable those throws were, QB Rating per Target measures the actual box-score outcome of targeting that receiver."}
{"question": "What Are Left-Aligned Targets?", "answer": "Left-aligned targets count how many passes were thrown to a receiver on plays where he lined up on the offense's left -- the side determined by where the quarterback is looking downfield, not by which sideline is physically closer to either broadcast camera. Side of field is a real, checkable tendency: roughly a third of NFL outside corners are true left/right players who travel to a side rather than following one receiver, so a heavy left-side target share can point straight at a favorable or unfavorable weekly matchup. This is a volume number, so read it beside Left-Aligned Target Rate, which controls for how many left-side routes he actually ran. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order rather than read from a labeled field, and resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes; they are an independent split from Slot & Perimeter+ next door and do not sum with it, or on their own, to a receiver's full route total."}
{"question": "What Are Right-Aligned Targets?", "answer": "Right-aligned targets count how many passes were thrown to a receiver on plays where he lined up on the offense's right -- the offense's right as the quarterback sees it, the same convention used on the coverage boards for defenders. A receiver's left and right target totals are not required to match; plenty of receivers, and especially ones who travel with a scheme rather than a boundary, show a clear lean to one side. Pair this with Right-Aligned Target Rate to separate a receiver who is heavily deployed to the right from one who is simply trusted when he gets there. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order, resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes, and are independent of the Slot & Perimeter+ split -- neither pair of numbers sums to a receiver's full route total."}
{"question": "What Are Left-Aligned Receptions?", "answer": "Left-aligned receptions count the catches a receiver made on plays where he lined up on the offense's left. Comparing this against Left-Aligned Targets is a rough read on how catchable a receiver's left-side work is, and comparing his left and right reception totals against each other can surface a real side preference -- about a third of starting corners are side-loyal rather than receiver-loyal, so a receiver's production can genuinely differ by side depending on who he draws there most weeks. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order, not read from a labeled field, and resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes. They are independent of the Slot & Perimeter+ split next door; left, right, slot and perimeter totals do not sum to a receiver's full route count."}
{"question": "What Are Right-Aligned Receptions?", "answer": "Right-aligned receptions count the catches a receiver made on plays where he lined up on the offense's right. Read next to Right-Aligned Targets for a catch-rate signal on that side, and next to the left-side board to see whether a receiver's production is genuinely split or concentrated on one side of the field. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes; the split is independent of Slot & Perimeter+, and neither axis, nor both together, accounts for a receiver's entire route total."}
{"question": "What Is Left-Aligned Target Rate?", "answer": "Left-aligned target rate is the percentage of a receiver's routes run on the offense's left that ended with a pass thrown his way. Dividing by routes from that side rather than by snaps or team attempts isolates trust from opportunity: a receiver who is targeted at a high rate on the left is the read there, independent of how often the offense actually lines him up on that side. Read it beside Right-Aligned Target Rate rather than assuming the two should match -- side preference is real for both receivers and the corners who cover them. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes; this split is independent of Slot Target Rate on Slot & Perimeter+ and the two do not combine into one number."}
{"question": "What Is Right-Aligned Target Rate?", "answer": "Right-aligned target rate is the percentage of a receiver's routes run on the offense's right that ended with a pass thrown his way. It is the same definition as Left-Aligned Target Rate and as Target Rate on the Cornerback+ pages, dividing by the routes actually run from that side rather than by snaps or overall attempts, so a receiver's right-side rate and a defender's rate allowed on the coverage boards read directly against each other. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order, resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes, and are independent of the slot/perimeter split -- neither the two sides nor the two alignments add up to a whole."}
{"question": "What Is Left-Aligned FP/Route?", "answer": "Left-Aligned FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points a receiver scored from left-side alignments divided by the left-side routes he ran. It folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one number and controls for how much of his work actually came from that side, which is what makes it useful for spotting a real side preference rather than assuming symmetry. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order, resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes, and are computed independently of Slot & Perimeter+ -- production from left, right, slot and perimeter does not combine into a receiver's full total."}
{"question": "What Is Right-Aligned FP/Route?", "answer": "Right-Aligned FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points a receiver scored from right-side alignments divided by the right-side routes he ran. Read against the left-side board, the two together show whether a receiver's production is genuinely built on one side of the field -- a real signal given how often outside corners are side-loyal rather than receiver-loyal. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order, resolve on about 98% of WR/slot routes, and are independent of Slot & Perimeter+; neither split accounts for a receiver's whole route total on its own."}
{"question": "What Are Slot Targets?", "answer": "Slot targets count how many passes were thrown to a receiver on plays where he lined up in the slot, inside the outermost receiver rather than out on the boundary. Slot work tends to draw a nickel corner, a linebacker or a safety instead of a team's best outside cornerback, and it comes with a free release and a two-way go, so a high slot target count usually points to a receiver an offense wants running option routes into space on early downs and third down. Read it beside Slot Target Rate, which controls for how often he actually ran a slot route. Slot and perimeter totals do not add up to a receiver's whole workload: snaps taken from the backfield or in-line are counted in neither."}
{"question": "What Are Perimeter Targets?", "answer": "Perimeter targets -- outside targets -- count how many passes were thrown to a receiver on plays where he lined up on the boundary rather than in the slot. Outside alignments are where press coverage and a team's top cornerback live, so perimeter volume says something different from slot volume: it is the harder, more contested workload, and the receivers who carry a lot of it are usually the ones an offense trusts to win one-on-one without scheme help. This board combines every outside alignment; for the independent left/right split, see Left & Right Alignment+. Slot and perimeter totals do not sum to a receiver's full workload -- backfield and in-line snaps belong to neither."}
{"question": "What Are Slot Receptions?", "answer": "Slot receptions count the catches a receiver made on plays where he lined up in the slot. Because slot routes are generally shorter and thrown against softer inside coverage, catch rates from the slot run higher than from the boundary, which is why slot receptions matter most in full-PPR and half-PPR formats where the reception itself scores. Compared against Slot Targets, the gap between the two is a rough read on how catchable a receiver's slot work is. Slot and perimeter do not account for all of a receiver's snaps -- backfield and in-line alignments are excluded from both."}
{"question": "What Are Perimeter Receptions?", "answer": "Perimeter receptions count the catches a receiver made on plays where he lined up outside rather than in the slot. Boundary catches are typically contested, thrown against a cornerback in press or off-man with the sideline as an extra defender, so catch rates here sit below slot rates for almost every receiver in the league. A player with heavy perimeter receptions is winning the version of the job that does not come with scheme help. Slot and perimeter do not cover a receiver's entire route tree -- backfield and in-line snaps are excluded from both boards."}
{"question": "What Is Slot Target Rate?", "answer": "Slot target rate is the percentage of a receiver's slot routes that ended with a pass thrown his way. Because it divides by routes rather than snaps or team pass attempts, it separates a receiver who is genuinely the read when he is inside from one who simply plays a lot of slot snaps -- volume boards reward the second, this one only rewards the first. It is the same definition used for defenders on the Cornerback+ pages, so a receiver's slot target rate and the rate a corner allows can be read against each other directly. Rates from the slot and from the perimeter are computed independently and are not shares of one whole."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter Target Rate?", "answer": "Perimeter target rate is the percentage of a receiver's outside routes that ended with a pass thrown his way. Dividing by routes rather than snaps is what makes it a measure of trust rather than deployment: a receiver can lead the league in boundary snaps and still rank poorly here if the quarterback is not looking his way. Outside rates generally run below slot rates, because boundary routes take longer to develop and are thrown into tighter coverage, so compare a receiver to other perimeter players rather than across the two boards. Slot and perimeter rates are each computed against their own route total and are not two halves of one number."}
{"question": "What Is Slot FP/Route?", "answer": "Slot FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points a receiver scored from slot alignments divided by the slot routes he ran. Fantasy points per route run is the standard way to compare receivers whose playing time differs, and splitting it by alignment shows where a player's production actually comes from: two receivers with identical season totals can look nothing alike once you see that one earns his points inside against a nickel and the other earns his on the boundary. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Points scored from backfield or in-line alignments appear on neither this board nor the perimeter one."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter FP/Route?", "answer": "Perimeter FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points a receiver scored from outside alignments divided by the perimeter routes he ran. Boundary work produces fewer catches but longer ones, so a receiver's perimeter figure is usually built on yardage and touchdowns rather than reception volume -- which makes this the board to read when deciding whether a big-play profile is real or the product of one long score. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Production from backfield or in-line alignments is excluded here and from the slot board alike."}
{"question": "What Are Routes Defended?", "answer": "Routes Defended records the total number of routes a CB covers in pass defense. This NFL player stat is a workload-based NFL advanced stat for measuring coverage involvement. In NFL team stats, it shows which players are most active in defending passing plays."}
{"question": "What Are Routes Faced vs. Man?", "answer": "Routes Faced vs. Man records the total number of routes a CB covers in man-to-man defense. This NFL player stat is a man-coverage workload NFL advanced stat. In NFL team stats, it helps identify cornerbacks most relied upon in man assignments."}
{"question": "What Are Routes Faced vs. Zone?", "answer": "Routes Faced vs. Zone records the total number of routes a CB covers in zone defense. This NFL player stat is a zone-coverage workload NFL advanced stat. In NFL team stats, it highlights players central to zone schemes."}
{"question": "What Is Man Coverage Rate?", "answer": "Man Coverage Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB is in man-to-man defense. This NFL player stat is a scheme-usage NFL advanced stat that reflects defensive strategy. In NFL team stats, it’s useful for understanding how often a defense relies on man coverage."}
{"question": "What Is Zone Coverage Rate?", "answer": "Zone Coverage Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB is in zone defense. This NFL player stat is a scheme-usage NFL advanced stat for coverage style. In NFL team stats, it’s key for understanding defensive philosophy."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 0 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 0 Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces Cover 0 — all-out man press with no deep safety. High Cover 0 Rate indicates a CB who is frequently tested in pressure packages."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 1 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 1 Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces Cover 1 — man coverage with a single high safety. It is one of the most common coverage shells a CB operates in."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 2 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 2 Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces Cover 2 zone. CBs in Cover 2 typically play underneath and funnel routes to the safeties."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 3 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 3 Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces Cover 3 — a three-deep zone with one high safety and two cornerbacks protecting the deep thirds."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 4 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 4 Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces Cover 4 (Quarters) — a four-deep zone with two safeties and two corners each responsible for a deep quarter of the field."}
{"question": "What Is Cover 6 Rate?", "answer": "Cover 6 Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces Cover 6 — a hybrid of Cover 2 to one side and Cover 4 to the other, giving corners different responsibilities depending on their alignment."}
{"question": "What Is Single High Rate?", "answer": "Single High Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces a single-high safety look (Cover 1 or Cover 3). Single-high coverages typically ask CBs to play with more isolation responsibility."}
{"question": "What Is Two High Rate?", "answer": "Two High Rate measures the percentage of coverage snaps where a CB faces a two-high safety shell (Cover 2, Cover 4, or Cover 6). Two-high coverages provide more over-the-top help for corners."}
{"question": "What Are Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Targets allowed count the total pass targets against a cornerback in coverage."}
{"question": "What Is Left Coverage Rate?", "answer": "Left coverage rate measures the share of a defender's routes defended where the receiver he was covering lined up on the offense's left. Sides are named from the offense's point of view throughout, so a corner with a high left coverage rate is physically stationed on the defense's right. Read it as a travel indicator: corners near the extremes stay on one side of the formation every snap, while a figure close to even means the defender either follows a particular receiver across the field or rotates through the slot. Left and right coverage rate add to very close to 100%: the denominator is the same charted receiver-alignment population the numerators come from, and the roughly one percent shortfall is routes on plays whose formation does not resolve a side, excluded from both rather than guessed."}
{"question": "What Is Right Coverage Rate?", "answer": "Right coverage rate measures the share of a defender's routes defended where the receiver he was covering lined up on the offense's right. Sides are named from the offense's point of view throughout, so a corner with a high right coverage rate is physically stationed on the defense's left. Read it beside left coverage rate: a defender near either extreme is a locked-side corner who stays put, while a more even split means he travels with a receiver or moves inside to the slot. The two add to very close to 100%, because both divide by the same charted receiver-alignment population; the roughly one percent shortfall is routes whose formation does not resolve a side, excluded from both rather than assigned to one."}
{"question": "What Is Target Rate?", "answer": "Target rate measures the percentage of routes defended that result in a target."}
{"question": "What Is Target Rate vs. Man?", "answer": "Target rate allowed vs. man measures the percentage of man coverage routes that result in a target."}
{"question": "What Is Target Rate vs. Zone?", "answer": "Target rate allowed vs. zone measures the percentage of zone coverage routes that result in a target."}
