{"question": "What Is Passer Rating Allowed?", "answer": "Passer Rating Allowed measures opposing QB passer rating when targeting a CB. This NFL player stat is a composite NFL advanced stat for evaluating overall coverage success. In NFL team stats, it’s one of the best single-number measures of defensive back performance."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Allowed Per Game?", "answer": "Fantasy Points Allowed per Game measures average fantasy points scored by receivers against a CB per game. This NFL player stat is a fantasy-impact NFL advanced stat for gauging weekly matchup strength. In NFL team stats, it’s used in scouting and fantasy game planning."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Per Target (Allowed)?", "answer": "Fantasy Points per Target (Allowed) measures average fantasy points scored by receivers per target against a CB. This NFL player stat is a per-target NFL advanced stat for quantifying opponent production efficiency. In NFL team stats, it identifies which defenders give up the most value per throw."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Allowed per Cover Snap?", "answer": "Fantasy Points Allowed per Cover Snap measures average fantasy points scored by receivers per coverage snap against a CB. This NFL player stat is a fantasy-focused NFL advanced stat for measuring matchup difficulty. In NFL team stats, it helps predict wide receiver production against specific defenders."}
{"question": "What Are Touchdowns Allowed?", "answer": "Touchdowns Allowed counts the total touchdowns a CB surrenders in coverage -- the clearest single marker of coverage breakdowns that turn into scores. It pairs with the man/zone splits and the TD Rate family to show whether a CB's scoring problem is volume-driven or tied to a specific coverage assignment, and it is a quick read for fantasy managers sizing up a receiver's matchup."}
{"question": "What Is TD Rate Allowed?", "answer": "TD Rate Allowed measures the percentage of routes a CB defends that end in a touchdown, isolating how often coverage snaps turn into scores rather than looking at raw touchdown totals."}
{"question": "What Are Touchdowns Allowed vs. Man?", "answer": "Touchdowns Allowed vs. Man counts the total touchdowns a CB surrenders while in man-to-man coverage. This NFL player stat is a man-coverage NFL advanced stat for isolating scoring breakdowns tied to man assignments."}
{"question": "What Are Touchdowns Allowed vs. Zone?", "answer": "Touchdowns Allowed vs. Zone counts the total touchdowns a CB surrenders while in zone coverage. This NFL player stat is a zone-coverage NFL advanced stat for isolating scoring breakdowns tied to zone assignments."}
{"question": "What Is TD Rate Allowed vs. Man?", "answer": "TD Rate Allowed vs. Man measures the percentage of man coverage routes a CB defends that end in a touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is TD Rate Allowed vs. Zone?", "answer": "TD Rate Allowed vs. Zone measures the percentage of zone coverage routes a CB defends that end in a touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is Yards After Catch Allowed?", "answer": "Yards After Catch Allowed records the total yards receivers pick up after the catch against a CB, the defensive mirror of the site's offensive Yards After Catch stat. It pairs with the Explosive Receptions family to flag CBs who allow big plays after the catch rather than just downfield."}
{"question": "What Is Yards After Catch Allowed per Reception?", "answer": "Yards After Catch Allowed per Reception divides a CB's Yards After Catch Allowed by their Receptions Allowed, the defensive rate mirror of the site's offensive Yards After Catch per Reception. It is distinct from Yards per Reception Allowed, which measures total yards (air yards plus YAC) per catch rather than just the after-the-catch portion."}
{"question": "What Are Explosive Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Explosive Receptions Allowed counts every reception of 20+ yards a CB gives up, the defensive mirror of the site's offensive Explosive Receptions stat. Viewed together, the pair frames the WR/CB matchup for big-play risk."}
{"question": "What Is Explosive Reception Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Explosive Reception Rate Allowed measures how often a CB's routes defended turn into a 20+ yard reception, pairing with the offensive Explosive Receptions family to frame big-play risk on a per-route basis."}
{"question": "What Is 10+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate?", "answer": "10+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate is the entry-level rung of the reception-threshold ladder (10/30/40/50+): because 10 yards clears on most completed passes, this is a high-volume baseline that mainly measures overall leakiness in coverage rather than true explosive-play risk, which the 30+, 40+, and 50+ rungs isolate."}
{"question": "What Is 30+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate?", "answer": "30+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate is the second rung of the reception-threshold ladder (10/30/40/50+), stepping past the standard 20-yard Explosive Reception line to isolate the meaningfully bigger chunk plays a CB gives up."}
{"question": "What Is 40+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate?", "answer": "40+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate is the third rung of the reception-threshold ladder (10/30/40/50+), tracking rare deep-shot breakdowns rather than routine coverage -- the kind of boom-or-bust plays that can flip a game."}
{"question": "What Is 50+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate?", "answer": "50+ Yard Receptions Allowed Rate is the final, most extreme rung of the reception-threshold ladder (10/30/40/50+), isolating the rarest coverage busts -- true home-run plays that almost always result from a blown assignment rather than ordinary coverage."}
{"question": "What Is Average Cushion Allowed?", "answer": "Average Cushion Allowed measures the average pre-snap yards between a CB and the receiver they line up against, the defensive mirror of the site's offensive Average Cushion. The average is taken over pass snaps where a cushion is charted, so plays without a charted cushion do not dilute the number."}
{"question": "What Is Average Depth of Target Allowed?", "answer": "Average Depth of Target Allowed (aDOT Allowed) measures the average target distance -- how far downfield a CB is targeted, on average -- the defensive mirror of the site's offensive Average Depth of Target. It divides total air yards allowed by Targets Allowed, matching that stat's all-targets (completed or not) filter."}
