{"question": "What Are Left-Aligned Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Left-aligned targets allowed counts how many passes were thrown at the receiver a defender was covering when that receiver lined up on the OFFENSE's left -- which is the defense's right side of the field. About a third of starting outside corners travel with a receiver rather than sticking to one side, but most do not: across 2025, of 97 defenders with at least 200 charted perimeter coverage snaps, the average share taken from his more common side was 66.7%, and 30 of the 97 topped 70%. A defender who is genuinely side-loyal will show a real gap between his left and right totals; one who travels will look similar on both. This is a volume number, so pair it with Left-Aligned Target Rate Allowed, which controls for how many left-side routes he actually defended."}
{"question": "What Are Right-Aligned Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Right-aligned targets allowed counts how many passes were thrown at the receiver a defender was covering when that receiver lined up on the OFFENSE's right -- which is the defense's left side of the field. Read next to Left-Aligned Targets Allowed to see whether a defender's workload is genuinely split by side or concentrated on one -- most true left/right corners show a clear lean, since they are drawn to a boundary rather than to a receiver. Pair this with Right-Aligned Target Rate Allowed before concluding anything from volume alone."}
{"question": "What Are Left-Aligned Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Left-aligned receptions allowed counts the catches surrendered by a defender when the receiver he was covering lined up on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's right side of the field. The gap between a defender's left-aligned targets allowed and left-aligned receptions allowed is a read on whether he is actually contesting throws to that side or merely present for them. Compare it to his right-side number to see whether a defender is genuinely better on one side of the field than the other."}
{"question": "What Are Right-Aligned Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Right-aligned receptions allowed counts the catches surrendered by a defender when the receiver he was covering lined up on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's left side of the field. Read alongside the left-side board; a defender who is stout on one side and soft on the other has a real, exploitable tendency, since roughly a third of starting corners are side-loyal by scheme rather than following a single receiver around the formation."}
{"question": "What Is Left-Aligned Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Left-aligned target rate allowed is the percentage of routes a defender covered on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's right side of the field -- that ended with a pass thrown his way. Dividing by routes defended from that side rather than by snaps turns volume into a trust signal: it answers whether quarterbacks attack that side of the field against this defender specifically, independent of how much time he spends there. The defenders at the bottom of this board are the ones offenses decide not to test on that side, a compliment that never shows up in yards or touchdowns allowed. It uses the same definition as Target Rate on Cornerback+ and as Left-Aligned Target Rate on the receiver boards, so all three read against each other directly."}
{"question": "What Is Right-Aligned Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Right-aligned target rate allowed is the percentage of routes a defender covered on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's left side of the field -- that ended with a pass thrown his way. Read it against the left-side rate rather than assuming symmetry: a defender's willingness-to-test signal can differ sharply by side, which is the whole reason this axis exists separately from the slot/perimeter one. It uses the same definition as Target Rate on Cornerback+, so a defender's rate here and a receiver's rate on the offense side are directly comparable."}
{"question": "What Is Left-Aligned FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Left-Aligned FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points surrendered by a defender divided by the routes he covered when the receiver lined up on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's right side of the field. It folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one figure and controls for workload on that specific side, which is what makes it usable for weekly matchup calls rather than only describing a season after the fact. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is Right-Aligned FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Right-Aligned FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points surrendered by a defender divided by the routes he covered when the receiver lined up on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's left side of the field. Together with the left-side board, this is how you tell whether a defender is actually vulnerable on one side of the field: a corner can be excellent when the offense works to his side and still be the wrong matchup when it works away from him. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Are Slot Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Slot targets allowed counts how many passes were thrown at the receiver a defender was covering when that receiver lined up in the slot. Inside coverage is mostly the nickel's job, and it is a different assignment from playing the boundary -- more traffic, more option routes, less sideline help. This is a volume number, so a defender who plays every snap will sit high on it whether or not he is actually being picked on; pair it with Slot Target Rate Allowed, which controls for how many slot routes he defended."}
{"question": "What Are Perimeter Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter targets allowed counts how many passes were thrown at the receiver a defender was covering when that receiver lined up outside. Boundary coverage is what outside corners are paid for, and volume here says as much about scheme as about the player: a corner who travels with a number one will see fewer throws than one left on whichever receiver lines up to his side. Read alongside Perimeter Target Rate Allowed before drawing a conclusion."}
{"question": "What Are Slot Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Slot receptions allowed counts the catches surrendered by a defender when the receiver he was covering lined up in the slot. Because inside routes are short and thrown into softer coverage, completion rates are high against everyone, so the gap between a defender's slot targets allowed and slot receptions allowed is the more telling number -- it is a read on whether he is actually contesting the throw or merely present for it."}
{"question": "What Are Perimeter Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter receptions allowed counts the catches surrendered by a defender when the receiver he was covering lined up outside. Boundary throws are contested far more often than slot throws, so completion rates against outside corners sit below inside rates across the league -- compare a defender to others playing the same alignment rather than across the two boards."}
{"question": "What Is Slot Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Slot target rate allowed is the percentage of slot routes a defender covered that ended with a pass thrown his way. Dividing by routes defended rather than by snaps turns a volume number into a signal about trust: it answers whether quarterbacks choose to attack this man when he is inside, independent of how often he was on the field. The defenders at the bottom of this board are the ones offenses decide not to test, which is a compliment that never appears in yards or touchdowns allowed. It uses the same definition as Target Rate on the Cornerback+ pages and as Slot Target Rate on the receiver boards, so all three read against each other directly."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter target rate allowed is the percentage of outside routes a defender covered that ended with a pass thrown his way. It is the cleanest single read on whether quarterbacks are willing to challenge a boundary corner, and the lowest figures on this board usually belong to the players offenses actively avoid. Outside rates run below slot rates league-wide, so judge a defender against others playing the same alignment."}
{"question": "What Is Slot FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Slot FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points surrendered by a defender divided by the slot routes he covered. This is the matchup number at the individual level: it folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one figure and controls for workload, which is what makes it usable for deciding whether a slot-heavy receiver has a good matchup this week rather than merely describing a season after the fact. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points surrendered by a defender divided by the perimeter routes he covered. Together with the slot board it tells you which kind of receiver a defender is actually vulnerable to -- a corner can be excellent on the boundary and still be the wrong man to leave inside. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is Fantasy Points Per Snap?", "answer": "Fantasy Points Per Snap measures the average fantasy points a player produces for every offensive snap he is on the field, regardless of whether the ball comes his way. This NFL player stat is a fantasy-efficiency NFL advanced stat for showing production relative to playing time. In NFL team stats, it highlights players who make the most of every snap rather than just the ones who touch the ball."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Per Opportunity?", "answer": "Fantasy Points Per Opportunity measures the average fantasy points a player produces per rushing attempt or target -- no passing attempts included. This NFL player stat is a fantasy-efficiency NFL advanced stat for showing scoring per chance. In NFL team stats, it highlights players who maximize their touches."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Per Touch?", "answer": "Fantasy Points Per Touch measures the average fantasy points a player produces per rushing attempt or reception. This NFL player stat is a ball-handling NFL advanced stat for production efficiency. In NFL team stats, it’s used to evaluate players’ per-touch value."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Per Dropback?", "answer": "Fantasy Points per Dropback measures the average fantasy points a quarterback produces per passing dropback, including rushing plays from scrambles. This NFL player stat is a passing-efficiency NFL advanced stat with rushing upside. In NFL team stats, it reflects QB dual-threat value."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Per Route Run?", "answer": "Fantasy Points per Route Run measures the average receiving fantasy points generated for every route a player runs. This NFL player stat is a route-efficiency NFL advanced stat for receiving production. In NFL team stats, it identifies receivers who deliver high output per route."}
{"question": "What Are Fantasy Points Per Target?", "answer": "Fantasy Points per Target measures the average receiving fantasy points generated for each target a player receives. This NFL player stat is a target-efficiency NFL advanced stat for converting looks into production. In NFL team stats, it shows receiving efficiency."}
{"question": "What Are Total Fantasy Points?", "answer": "Total Fantasy Points records the cumulative fantasy points a player has scored over a defined period. This NFL player stat is a total-output NFL advanced stat for overall production. In NFL team stats, it's a measure of total scoring contribution."}
{"question": "What Is Standard Scoring?", "answer": "Standard Scoring refers to a fantasy football scoring format where players earn points primarily from yardage and touchdowns, without reception bonuses. This fantasy format favors touchdown-heavy players. In NFL fantasy stats, it's the baseline for comparing league formats."}
{"question": "What Is Half-Point PPR Scoring?", "answer": "Half-Point PPR Scoring refers to a scoring format where each reception earns 0.5 fantasy points in addition to yardage and touchdown scoring. This fantasy format is widely used across fantasy football platforms. In NFL fantasy stats, it balances the value of receptions with yardage-based scoring."}
{"question": "What Is Underdog Fantasy Points Per Game?", "answer": "Underdog Fantasy Points Per Game tracks player performance using Underdog Fantasy's half-PPR scoring system, where each reception earns 0.5 fantasy points in addition to yardage and touchdown scoring. This metric is essential for Underdog Fantasy contests including Best Ball and Pick'em games."}
{"question": "What Is Full PPR?", "answer": "Full PPR refers to a scoring format where each reception earns 1 fantasy point in addition to yardage and touchdown scoring. This fantasy format favors high-volume pass-catchers. In NFL fantasy stats, it's the most reception-friendly system."}
{"question": "What Are DraftKings Fantasy Points Per Game?", "answer": "DraftKings, Fantasy Points per Game measures the average fantasy points a player scores per game using DraftKings scoring rules, which include full PPR and specific yardage bonuses. This NFL fantasy stat is platform-specific for DFS analysis."}
{"question": "What Are FanDuel Fantasy Points Per Game?", "answer": "FanDuel Fantasy Points per Game measures the average fantasy points a player scores per game using FanDuel scoring rules, which include half-PPR and different yardage values than DraftKings. This NFL fantasy stat is platform-specific for DFS play."}
{"question": "What Are NFFC Fantasy Points Per Game?", "answer": "NFFC, Fantasy Points per Game measures the average fantasy points a player scores per game in NFFC leagues, typically using full PPR scoring with specific bonuses. This NFL fantasy stat is format-specific and valuable for redraft and best ball strategy."}
{"question": "What Are FFPC Fantasy Points Per Game?", "answer": "FFPC, Fantasy Points per Game measures the average fantasy points a player scores per game using FFPC scoring rules -- full PPR with TE Premium, where tight ends earn an extra 0.5 points per reception (1.5 total) while every other position scores standard full-PPR, plus -1 per interception and fumble lost. This NFL fantasy stat is platform-specific and valuable for FFPC redraft, best ball, and Dynasty leagues, where TE Premium shifts tight end value more than any other scoring rule on the site. What is Fantasy Points per Target vs Zone? Fantasy Points per Target vs Zone measures the average fantasy points generated per target against zone coverage. This NFL player stat is a zone-defense NFL advanced stat for efficiency versus space-based coverage. In NFL team stats, it reflects situational production. What is Fantasy Points per Target vs Man? Fantasy Points per Target vs Man measures the average fantasy points generated per target against man coverage. This NFL player stat is a man-defense NFL advanced stat for effectiveness against tight coverage. In NFL team stats, it shows matchup-specific efficiency."}
