{"question": "What Are Opponent Penalty First Downs Per Game?", "answer": "Opponent penalty first downs per game measures the average number of first downs a team earns due to opponent penalties. This stat reflects opponent mistakes benefiting the offense."}
{"question": "What Are Penalty Yards Per Penalty?", "answer": "Penalty yards per penalty measures the average yardage lost per penalty committed. This stat reflects the severity of infractions."}
{"question": "What Are Opponent Penalty Yards Per Penalty?", "answer": "Opponent penalty yards per penalty measures the average yardage opponents lose per penalty committed. This stat reflects severity of opponent infractions."}
{"question": "What Are Opponent Penalties Per Play?", "answer": "Opponent penalties per play measures the frequency of opponent penalties relative to their total plays run. This stat reflects opponent discipline issues."}
{"question": "What Are Team Left-Aligned WR Targets?", "answer": "Team left-aligned WR targets count how many passes an offense threw to wide receivers lined up on its own left side of the field, as the quarterback sees it looking downfield. Offenses lean toward a side for real reasons -- a receiver's alignment habits, a favorable weekly matchup, or simply where the better blocker lines up on run-pass options -- so a heavy left-side share is a genuine tendency, not noise. It is a volume number, so read it beside Team Left-Aligned Target Rate, which controls for how many left-side routes the offense actually ran. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and are independent of the Slot & Perimeter Offense+ split; neither axis, nor both together, accounts for a team's entire passing game."}
{"question": "What Are Team Right-Aligned WR Targets?", "answer": "Team right-aligned WR targets count how many passes an offense threw to wide receivers lined up on its own right side of the field. Compared against the left-side board, a clear gap between the two says something real about how a team's passing game is built -- most offenses are not perfectly symmetric, and neither are the corners defending them. Pair this with Team Right-Aligned Target Rate before drawing a conclusion from volume alone. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and are independent of Slot & Perimeter Offense+; the two axes do not sum to one another or to a team's whole passing game."}
{"question": "What Are Team Left-Aligned WR Receptions?", "answer": "Team left-aligned WR receptions count the catches an offense produced from receivers lined up on its own left side of the field. The gap between a team's left-aligned targets and left-aligned receptions is a rough read on how catchable that side of its passing game is. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and are independent of the Slot & Perimeter Offense+ split -- left, right, slot and perimeter totals do not sum to a team's whole passing game."}
{"question": "What Are Team Right-Aligned WR Receptions?", "answer": "Team right-aligned WR receptions count the catches an offense produced from receivers lined up on its own right side of the field. Read next to the left-side board to see whether an offense's production is genuinely split by side or concentrated on one. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and are independent of Slot & Perimeter Offense+; neither split accounts for an offense's entire passing game."}
{"question": "What Is Team Left-Aligned WR Target Rate?", "answer": "Team left-aligned WR target rate is the percentage of an offense's left-side routes that ended with a pass thrown that way. Dividing by routes from that side rather than by total pass attempts is what separates intent from opportunity: a team can run a lot of left-side routes without actually throwing there, and only this board can tell the difference. It uses the same definition as the receiver and coverage boards, so an offense's rate here, a receiver's, and the rate a defender allows are all directly comparable. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and are independent of the Slot & Perimeter Offense+ split."}
{"question": "What Is Team Right-Aligned WR Target Rate?", "answer": "Team right-aligned WR target rate is the percentage of an offense's right-side routes that ended with a pass thrown that way. Read it against the left-side rate rather than assuming symmetry -- offenses are built around personnel, not geometry, and a real gap between the two sides is common. Left and right are derived from the charted formation order and are independent of the Slot & Perimeter Offense+ split; the two axes do not combine into one number."}
{"question": "What Is Team Left-Aligned WR FP/Route?", "answer": "Team Left-Aligned WR FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points an offense produced from left-side alignments divided by the left-side routes its receivers ran. It folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one number and controls for how much left-side work a team actually does, which is the cleanest read on whether that side of an offense's passing game is genuinely productive or merely busy. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Left and right are independent of the Slot & Perimeter Offense+ split and do not combine with it into one total."}
{"question": "What Is Team Right-Aligned WR FP/Route?", "answer": "Team Right-Aligned WR FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points an offense produced from right-side alignments divided by the right-side routes its receivers ran. Read against the left-side figure to see whether a team's passing production leans on one side of the field. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Are Team Slot WR Targets?", "answer": "Team slot WR targets count how many passes an offense threw to wide receivers lined up in the slot rather than out on the boundary. Offenses that live inside are usually built on timing and space -- quick game, option routes, and mismatches against a nickel or a linebacker -- while teams at the bottom of this board tend to ask their receivers to win outside instead. It is a volume number, so read it beside Team Slot Target Rate, which controls for how often those receivers actually ran slot routes. Targets to backfield and in-line alignments are counted on neither this board nor the perimeter one."}
{"question": "What Are Team Perimeter WR Targets?", "answer": "Team perimeter WR targets count how many passes an offense threw to wide receivers lined up outside rather than in the slot. Boundary throws are the harder ones -- into press coverage, against a team's best cornerback, with the sideline helping the defence -- so offenses that lean this way are usually confident in a receiver who can win alone. This board combines every outside alignment; for the independent left/right split, see Team Left-Aligned WR Targets on Left & Right Alignment Offense+. Slot and perimeter do not account for an offense's entire passing game."}
{"question": "What Are Team Slot WR Receptions?", "answer": "Team slot WR receptions count the catches an offense produced from receivers lined up in the slot. Inside routes are shorter and thrown against softer coverage, so completion rates from the slot run high across the league and this number tends to track slot volume closely. The gap between a team's slot targets and slot receptions is a rough read on how catchable its inside passing game is."}
{"question": "What Are Team Perimeter WR Receptions?", "answer": "Team perimeter WR receptions count the catches an offense produced from receivers lined up outside. Because boundary throws are contested more often than slot throws, completion rates here sit below inside rates for every team in the league, so compare offenses against each other on this board rather than against their own slot figures."}
{"question": "What Is Team Slot WR Target Rate?", "answer": "Team slot WR target rate is the percentage of an offense's slot routes that ended with a pass thrown inside. Dividing by routes rather than by pass attempts is what separates intent from opportunity: a team can run a lot of slot routes without actually throwing there, and only this board can tell the difference. It uses the same definition as the receiver and coverage boards, so an offense's slot target rate, a receiver's, and the rate a defender allows are all directly comparable."}
{"question": "What Is Team Perimeter WR Target Rate?", "answer": "Team perimeter WR target rate is the percentage of an offense's outside routes that ended with a pass thrown to the boundary. Outside rates run below slot rates league-wide, because boundary routes take longer to develop and are thrown into tighter coverage, so judge an offense against the other 31 here rather than against its own slot number."}
{"question": "What Is Team Slot WR FP/Route?", "answer": "Team slot WR FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points an offense produced from slot alignments divided by the slot routes its receivers ran. It folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one number and controls for how much slot work a team actually does, which makes it the cleanest read on whether an offense's inside passing game is genuinely productive or merely busy. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is Team Perimeter WR FP/Route?", "answer": "Team perimeter WR FP/Route is the half-PPR fantasy points an offense produced from outside alignments divided by the perimeter routes its receivers ran. Boundary work produces fewer catches but longer ones, so a team's perimeter figure is usually built on yardage and touchdowns rather than reception volume. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Are Defensive Expected Pressures?", "answer": "Defensive expected pressures estimates how many QB pressures a defense should have generated given how long opposing quarterbacks held the ball against it. Every play's expectation comes from Kaplan-Meier survival curves fit on league-wide time-to-pressure and time-to-throw data: the longer a quarterback holds the ball, the higher the league-wide odds that pressure arrives. Summing those per-play probabilities produces an opponent-adjusted baseline, so a defense that feasted on statuesque pocket passers is graded against that easier slate. A pass rush whose actual pressures forced beats this number is winning faster than the league does; one that trails it is being carried by opportunity rather than rush wins."}
{"question": "What Is Defensive Pressure Rate Over Expected?", "answer": "Defensive Pressure Rate Over Expected (POE) compares a defense's actual quarterback pressure rate created to an expected rate derived from Kaplan-Meier survival curves fit on league-wide time-to-pressure and time-to-throw data. Every dropback carries some baseline pressure probability simply from how long the quarterback holds the ball, so a pass rush facing an offense that throws quickly starts from a lower expected-pressure baseline than one facing an offense that holds the ball. Defensive POE isolates the pass rush's actual performance from that baseline: a positive POE means the defense creates more pressure than expected given the time it was given to get there, signaling an elite pass rush, while a negative POE means it generates less pressure than expected, exposing a front that isn't winning its share of one-on-ones. Because it adjusts for time to throw, defensive POE separates true pass-rush ability from opponent scheme (facing quick-game offenses looks worse, not better, once expectation is accounted for)."}
{"question": "What Is Defensive Stacked Box Rate?", "answer": "Defensive Stacked Box Rate is the percentage of a defense's opponent running back carries that are met with a stacked box -- a stacked box is eight or more defenders in the box at the snap. It is the team-defense mirror of the player-side Stacked Box Rate: how often this defense deploys extra numbers against the run, independent of how often opponents actually choose to run into it. What is Havoc Rate? Havoc rate is the percentage of defensive plays where the defense records a tackle for loss, forces a fumble, deflects a pass, or makes an interception. What is Offensive ARBY? Inspired by Aaron Schatz's Adjusted Line Yards, ARBY (Adjusted Run Blocking Yards) isolates how much of a team's rushing success comes from the offensive line, separating line-created yardage from what the running back generates on his own -- surfacing true outliers: elite lines, creative schemes, and running backs who maximize good blocking. For example, despite the Eagles' offensive line struggling with injuries and finishing 30th in Offensive ARBY in 2025, Philadelphia finished 15th in RB Yards/Carry, thanks to Saquon Barkley's unique ability to elude and break tackles."}
{"question": "What Are Offensive Expected Pressures Allowed?", "answer": "Offensive expected pressures allowed estimates how many QB pressures an offense should have surrendered given how long its quarterback held the ball on each dropback. Every play's expectation comes from Kaplan-Meier survival curves fit on league-wide time-to-pressure and time-to-throw data: the longer the ball is held, the higher the league-wide odds that pressure arrives. Summing those per-play probabilities produces a schedule- and scheme-adjusted baseline. An offense whose actual pressures allowed beats this number is out-protecting the league at its own time to throw; one that trails it is losing blocks faster than the league does."}
{"question": "What Is Offensive Pressure Rate Over Expected?", "answer": "Offensive Pressure Rate Over Expected (POE) compares a team's actual quarterback pressure rate allowed to an expected rate derived from Kaplan-Meier survival curves fit on league-wide time-to-pressure and time-to-throw data. Every dropback carries some baseline pressure risk simply from how long the quarterback holds the ball -- a three-second hold is pressured far more often league-wide than a one-second quick game throw. POE isolates the offensive line's actual performance from that baseline: a negative POE means the offensive line allows fewer pressures than expected given its quarterback's time to throw, signaling elite pass protection, while a positive POE means it allows more pressure than expected, exposing a line getting beaten regardless of how quickly the ball comes out. Because it adjusts for time to throw, POE separates true pass-protection quality from offensive scheme (quick-game offenses that rarely get pressured simply because they rarely hold the ball look worse, not better, once expectation is accounted for). What is Power Success Rate? Power success rate is the percentage of runs on third or fourth down with two yards or less to go, or on the goal line, that result in a first down or touchdown. What is Stuff Rate? Stuff rate is the percentage of a team's rushing plays that are stopped at or behind the line of scrimmage."}
{"question": "What Is TDs Allowed Per Pass in Coverage?", "answer": "Touchdowns allowed per pass in coverage measures the rate of touchdown passes given up per pass attempt in coverage. What are Touchdowns Allowed vs Zone Coverage? Touchdowns allowed vs zone coverage is the total number of receiving touchdowns a defense surrenders on plays where it is in zone coverage. It helps isolate communication and landmark issues that lead to scores. What are Touchdowns Allowed vs Man Coverage? Touchdowns allowed vs man coverage is the total number of receiving touchdowns a defense surrenders on plays where it is in man coverage. It captures one-on-one breakdowns and matchup losses that result in scores. What is Target Rate Allowed vs Man Coverage? Target rate allowed vs man coverage is the percentage of passing attempts that are targeted when a defense is in man coverage. It indicates how often opposing offenses choose to attack man-to-man matchups. What is Target Rate Allowed vs Zone Coverage? Target rate allowed vs zone coverage is the percentage of passing attempts that are targeted when a defense is in zone coverage. It shows how frequently opposing offenses choose to attack zone structures. What is Single High Rate? Single high rate is the percentage of a defense's classified coverage snaps played with one deep safety, the family that includes Cover 1 and Cover 3. Single high structures commit an extra defender near the line of scrimmage, so they tend to be stouter against the run and more vulnerable to the deep shot outside the numbers. What is Two High Rate? Two high rate is the percentage of a defense's classified coverage snaps played with two deep safeties, the family that includes Cover 2, Cover 4 and Cover 6. Two high structures trade run support for deep coverage, which tends to cap explosive passing plays while conceding underneath throws and rushing lanes. What is Cover 0 Rate? Cover 0 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played in man coverage with no deep safety help. It is the most aggressive shell in football, almost always paired with an extra rusher, and it trades a guaranteed one-on-one matchup for the offense against the chance to get home before the throw. What is Cover 1 Rate? Cover 1 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played in man coverage underneath one deep safety. It is the most common man structure in the league, and a high rate signals a defense that trusts its corners to win individual matchups while keeping a single defender over the top. What is Cover 2 Rate? Cover 2 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played in a two-deep zone, with each safety responsible for half the deep field and five defenders underneath. It protects the sideline and the deep ball while leaving the seam between the safeties and the space in front of the corners available. What is Cover 2M (Tampa) Rate? Cover 2M rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played with man coverage underneath a two-deep shell, the structure commonly called Tampa 2. It pairs the deep security of two high safeties with tighter underneath coverage than a standard Cover 2 zone, at the cost of leaving defenders in trail position on crossing routes. What is Cover 3 Rate? Cover 3 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played with the deep field split into thirds between two corners and a single high safety. It is the league's most common zone shell, strong against the deep ball and the run, and most often attacked underneath and on out-breaking routes at intermediate depth. What is Cover 4 (Quarters) Rate? Cover 4 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played in quarters, with four defenders splitting the deep field evenly. It is the most conservative common shell against explosive plays, and defenses that live in it typically concede short completions and rushing yardage in exchange for keeping everything in front of them. What is Cover 6 Rate? Cover 6 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played in a split-field shell, with quarters coverage to one side of the formation and Cover 2 to the other. Defenses use it to apply different rules to the strong and weak sides of a formation, often to take away one specific receiver or leverage a hash mark. What is Cover 9 Rate? Cover 9 rate is the percentage of a defense's coverage snaps played in a three-deep match structure, where defenders pattern-match receivers into their zones rather than dropping to fixed landmarks. It is a rarely charted shell, so single-season sample sizes are small and week-to-week swings should be read with caution."}
{"question": "What Are Left-Aligned WR Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Left-aligned WR targets allowed counts how many passes a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's own right side of the field. About a third of starting outside corners travel with a receiver rather than sticking to a side of the field, but most defenses run enough side-based coverage rules that a real gap between left and right volume is common and worth reading. This is a volume number, so pair it with Left-Aligned Target Rate Allowed before drawing a conclusion -- a defense that faces a lot of overall attempts will concede left-side targets whether or not that side is genuinely a weakness."}
{"question": "What Are Right-Aligned WR Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Right-aligned WR targets allowed counts how many passes a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's own left side of the field. Read next to the left-side board: a defense that concedes far more volume to one side than the other usually has an identifiable weak link there, whether that is a starting corner, a safety rotation, or a scheme choice. Pair it with Right-Aligned Target Rate Allowed before concluding anything from volume alone."}
{"question": "What Are Left-Aligned WR Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Left-aligned WR receptions allowed counts the catches a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's own right side of the field. The gap between a defense's left-aligned targets allowed and left-aligned receptions allowed is a decent read on whether that side is actually contesting throws or merely present for them. Read alongside Left-Aligned FP/Route Allowed, which accounts for what those catches were worth rather than only how many there were."}
{"question": "What Are Right-Aligned WR Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Right-aligned WR receptions allowed counts the catches a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's own left side of the field. Compare defenses to each other on this board rather than assuming a team's left and right numbers should match; side-specific weaknesses are common and this is one of the few boards built to surface them directly."}
{"question": "What Is Left-Aligned WR Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Left-aligned WR target rate allowed is the percentage of routes run against a defense on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's own right side of the field -- that ended with a pass thrown that way. Dividing by routes defended from that side rather than by total attempts turns a volume number into a signal: it answers whether quarterbacks choose that side when it is available, independent of how often the defense was throwing-down overall. A high rate is an invitation being accepted. It uses the same definition as Target Rate on Cornerback+ and as Left-Aligned Target Rate on the receiver side, so all three read against each other directly."}
{"question": "What Is Right-Aligned WR Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Right-aligned WR target rate allowed is the percentage of routes run against a defense on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's own left side of the field -- that ended with a pass thrown that way. It is the cleanest single read on whether quarterbacks are willing to challenge that side of a defense: the teams at the bottom of this board are the ones offenses decide not to test there, a compliment that never shows up in yards or touchdowns allowed. Judge a defense against the other 31 here rather than against its own left-side figure."}
{"question": "What Is Left-Aligned WR FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Left-Aligned WR FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points a defense conceded to receivers on the OFFENSE's left -- the defense's own right side of the field -- divided by the routes it defended there. This is the matchup number: it folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one figure and controls for how much left-side work a defense actually faced, which is what makes it usable for start-sit decisions rather than only for describing a season after the fact. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Is Right-Aligned WR FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Right-Aligned WR FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points a defense conceded to receivers on the OFFENSE's right -- the defense's own left side of the field -- divided by the routes it defended there. Together with the left-side board, this is how you tell which side of a defense is actually vulnerable: a defense can be stout on one side and still be the best matchup on the slate for a receiver who works to the other. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown."}
{"question": "What Are Slot WR Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Slot WR targets allowed counts how many passes a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up in the slot. Quarterbacks attack the slot when they do not like what the boundary corners are doing, so a high figure here usually says one of two things: the nickel is the weak point, or the defense plays enough zone that inside throws are simply the easy read. It is a volume number, so pair it with Slot Target Rate Allowed before drawing a conclusion -- a defense that faces a lot of pass attempts will concede slot targets whether or not the slot is a problem. Targets against backfield and in-line alignments are counted on neither this board nor the perimeter one."}
{"question": "What Are Perimeter WR Targets Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter WR targets allowed counts how many passes a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up outside rather than in the slot. Boundary targets are the ones a defense's top cornerbacks are paid to take away, so a defense conceding a lot of them is either being schemed away from its best coverage or does not have a corner opponents are willing to avoid. This board combines every outside alignment; for the independent left/right breakout, see Left-Aligned WR Targets Allowed on Left & Right Allowed+. Slot and perimeter do not account for everything a defense faced."}
{"question": "What Are Slot WR Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Slot WR receptions allowed counts the catches a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up in the slot. Because inside routes are shorter and thrown against softer coverage, completion rates on slot targets are high across the league, and the gap between a defense's slot targets allowed and slot receptions allowed is a decent read on whether its nickel is actually contesting anything. Read alongside Slot FP/Route Allowed, which accounts for what those catches were worth rather than only how many there were."}
{"question": "What Are Perimeter WR Receptions Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter WR receptions allowed counts the catches a defense conceded to wide receivers lined up outside. Boundary throws are contested more often than slot throws, so completion rates here are lower for every defense, and the teams that suppress this number are usually the ones with a corner who can play press without safety help. Compare defenses to each other on this board rather than against their own slot figures -- the two alignments are different jobs and the league-wide baselines are not the same."}
{"question": "What Is Slot WR Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Slot WR target rate allowed is the percentage of slot routes run against a defense that ended with a pass thrown. Dividing by routes defended rather than by pass attempts is what turns a volume number into a signal: it answers whether quarterbacks choose the slot when it is available, independent of how often the defense was throwing-down in the first place. A high rate is an invitation being accepted. It uses the same definition as Target Rate on the Cornerback+ pages and as Slot Target Rate on the receiver side, so all three can be read against each other directly."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter WR Target Rate Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter WR target rate allowed is the percentage of outside routes run against a defense that ended with a pass thrown. It is the cleanest single read on whether quarterbacks are willing to challenge a defense's boundary corners: the defenses at the bottom of this board are the ones offenses decide not to test, which is a compliment that never shows up in yards or touchdowns allowed. Outside rates run below slot rates league-wide, so judge a defense against the other 31 on this board rather than against its own slot figure."}
{"question": "What Is Slot WR FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Slot WR FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points a defense conceded to slot receivers divided by the slot routes it defended. This is the matchup number: it folds volume, catch rate, yardage and touchdowns into one figure and controls for how much slot work a defense actually faced, which is what makes it usable for start-sit decisions rather than only for describing a season after the fact. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Points conceded to backfield and in-line alignments appear on neither this board nor the perimeter one."}
{"question": "What Is Perimeter WR FP/Route Allowed?", "answer": "Perimeter WR FP/Route allowed is the half-PPR fantasy points a defense conceded to outside receivers divided by the perimeter routes it defended. It is the boundary counterpart to the slot board, and the two together are how you tell which kind of receiver a defense is actually vulnerable to -- a defense can be stout against outside alignments and still be the best matchup on the slate for a slot-heavy receiver. Scoring is 0.5 points per reception, 0.1 per receiving yard, and 6 per touchdown. Production against backfield and in-line alignments is excluded from both boards."}
{"question": "What Is Underdog Fantasy Points Allowed?", "answer": "Underdog Fantasy Points Allowed tracks how many fantasy points a defense surrenders using Underdog Fantasy's half-PPR scoring system, where each reception earns 0.5 fantasy points in addition to yardage and touchdown scoring. This metric is essential for identifying favorable matchups in Underdog Fantasy contests including Best Ball and Pick'em games."}
{"question": "What Are FanDuel Fantasy Points Per Game Allowed?", "answer": "Average FanDuel points allowed per game using FanDuel scoring rules. Helps compare defenses by how much FD production they typically give up to opponents. What is Standard Scoring Allowed? Fantasy points allowed under standard scoring rules, awarding 4 points per passing touchdown, 6 points per rushing or receiving touchdown, 1 point per 25 passing yards, and 1 point per 10 rushing or receiving yards, with no points for receptions. Highlights defenses that allow the most standard scoring production."}
{"question": "What Are DraftKings Fantasy Points Per Game Allowed?", "answer": "This metric reports the average DraftKings fantasy points a defense allows per game using DK scoring rules. It normalizes across opponents and weeks to identify defenses that consistently yield production. What is Fantasy Points Per Dropback Allowed? Average fantasy points allowed by a defense per opposing dropback. Useful for evaluating pass defense through a fantasy efficiency lens independent of volume. What is Fantasy Points Per Opportunity Allowed? Average fantasy points allowed per opponent opportunity, where an opportunity is a rush attempt or target. Helps separate efficiency allowed from raw volume. What is Fantasy Points Per Route Run Allowed? Average receiving fantasy points (PPR) surrendered per opponent route run. Useful for judging coverage efficiency regardless of target volume."}
{"question": "What Is Fantasy Points Per Target Allowed?", "answer": "Average fantasy points allowed per target against the defense. Highlights how costly an average target is versus this unit. What is Fantasy Points Per Touch Allowed? Average fantasy points allowed per opponent touch, where a touch is a rush attempt or reception. Useful for RB and WR matchup analysis."}
{"question": "What Is Broken Tackles Forced Per Attempt?", "answer": "Broken tackles forced per attempt calculates the rate at which tackle attempts are broken by opponents. What is Missed Tackle Percentage? Missed tackle percentage is the percentage of total tackle attempts a defender fails to complete. What is Pressure Rate? Pressure rate is the percentage of defensive pass rushes that result in pressure on the quarterback. What is Blitz Rate? Blitz rate is the percentage of defensive plays where extra pass rushers are sent beyond the standard pass rushers. What is QB Hit Percentage? Quarterback hit percentage is the percentage of dropbacks where the quarterback is hit while passing. What is QB Sack Percentage? Quarterback sack percentage is the percentage of dropbacks ending in a sack. What is Opponent Explosive Play Rate? Opponent explosive play rate is the percentage of plays where the opposing offense gains a large chunk of yards, typically 20+ passing yards or 10+ rushing yards."}
{"question": "What Is EPA Per Play?", "answer": "EPA per play is the average expected points added by a player on each play they are involved in. It accounts for down, distance, and field position to measure how much value a player generates relative to what would be expected in the same situation."}
{"question": "What Is Total EPA?", "answer": "Total EPA is the cumulative expected points added across all plays a player is involved in throughout the season — not points scored, but the aggregate margin above what would be expected given the same game situations. It reflects the total volume of value a player generated above average across all games."}
{"question": "What Is Passing EPA Per Play?", "answer": "Passing EPA per play is the average expected points added by a quarterback on each passing attempt. It captures how efficiently a passer creates value through the air by adjusting for game situation on every dropback."}
{"question": "What Is Total Passing EPA?", "answer": "Total passing EPA is the cumulative expected points added on all passing plays throughout the season. It reflects the aggregate passing value a quarterback generated above situational expectation across all games — not touchdowns or yards, but the total margin above average on every dropback."}
{"question": "What Is Rushing EPA Per Play?", "answer": "Rushing EPA per play is the average expected points added on rushing attempts for a ball carrier. It adjusts for game situation to measure how efficiently a rusher creates value on the ground relative to what would be expected."}
{"question": "What Is Total Rushing EPA?", "answer": "Total rushing EPA is the cumulative expected points added across all rushing plays throughout the season. A player with +20 Total Rushing EPA added roughly 20 expected points of value above average across all carries in all games that season."}
{"question": "What Is Receiving EPA Per Play?", "answer": "Receiving EPA per play is the average expected points added when a receiver is targeted. It measures how much value a pass catcher generates on each play they are involved in by adjusting for game situation."}
{"question": "What Is Total Receiving EPA?", "answer": "Total receiving EPA is the cumulative expected points added on all targets for a receiver throughout the season. It reflects the total value a pass catcher generated above situational expectation across all games — a high number means the player consistently created more value than expected on every play they were involved in."}
{"question": "What Is Success Rate?", "answer": "Success rate is the percentage of plays on which a player generates positive expected points added. A successful play gains enough yards to maintain a manageable down-and-distance based on the field position and situation."}
{"question": "What Are Field Goals Made?", "answer": "Field Goals Made record the total number of successful baskets a player scores during games. For instance, if a player hits 11 shots in a single game, their field goals made for that contest is 11. This is a core NBA player stat used to track offensive output and contribution to NBA scoring stats."}
{"question": "What Are Field Goals Attempted?", "answer": "Field Goals Attempted track the total number of shots a player takes, whether made or missed. For example, if a player attempts 20 shots but makes 8, their field goals attempted is still recorded as 20. Within NBA player stats, this reflects workload and offensive involvement, helping evaluate efficiency in NBA scoring stats. What is Three Point Percentage? Three Point Percentage measures the accuracy of a player’s three-point shots by dividing makes by attempts. For instance, a player who hits 4 of 10 three-pointers has a 40% rate. This is an essential NBA player stat for evaluating perimeter shooting and a core measure within NBA scoring stats. What are Three Point Field Goals Made? Three Point Field Goals Made count the total number of three-point shots a player scores. For example, if a player makes 6 threes in a game, their three-point field goals made is 6. This NBA player stat directly contributes to NBA scoring stats by highlighting long-range scoring ability. What are Three Point Field Goals Attempted? Three Point Field Goals Attempted record the total number of three-point shots a player tries. For example, if a player shoots 12 times from beyond the arc, their three-point attempts are 12 regardless of makes. This NBA player stat measures volume and role within NBA scoring stats. What is Two Point Percentage? Two Point Percentage calculates the success rate of shots taken inside the three-point line. For instance, a player who makes 7 of 14 two-point attempts has a 50% two-point percentage. This is a valuable NBA player stat that reveals efficiency inside the arc, crucial to NBA scoring stats. What are Two Point Field Goals Made? Two Point Field Goals Made record the total number of baskets scored inside the three-point line. For example, if a player makes 9 layups or mid-range shots, their two-point field goals made is 9. This NBA player stat is key in evaluating offensive production in NBA scoring stats. What are Two Point Field Goals Attempted? Two Point Field Goals Attempted track the total number of two-point shots taken in a game or season. For instance, a player who shoots 15 times inside the arc has 15 two-point attempts. This NBA player stat helps balance efficiency against volume within NBA scoring stats. What is Free Throw Percentage? Free Throw Percentage measures the accuracy of free throws by dividing makes by attempts. For example, a player who makes 8 of 10 free throws has an 80% rate. This NBA player stat highlights reliability at the line and is an important factor in NBA scoring stats."}
{"question": "What Are Free Throws Made?", "answer": "Free Throws Made count the total number of successful free throw shots. For instance, if a player makes 6 free throws in one game, their free throws made is 6. This NBA player stat reflects scoring consistency and adds to overall NBA scoring stats."}
{"question": "What Are Free Throws Attempted?", "answer": "Free Throws Attempted record the number of free throw shots a player takes. For example, if a player goes 7-for-10 at the line, their free throw attempts are recorded as 10. This NBA player stat reflects a player’s ability to draw fouls and is closely tied to NBA scoring stats. What is Effective Field Goal Percentage (eFG%)? Effective Field Goal Percentage (eFG%) adjusts field goal percentage to account for the added value of three-pointers. For example, a player making 4 two-pointers and 2 threes out of 10 shots has a higher eFG% than their basic percentage suggests. This advanced NBA player stat provides better insight into efficiency in NBA scoring stats. What is True Shooting Percentage (TS%)? True Shooting Percentage (TS%) measures scoring efficiency by including field goals, three-pointers, and free throws in one formula. For example, if a player scores 30 points on 20 total shooting possessions, their TS% reflects that balance. This NBA player stat offers a complete view of efficiency within NBA scoring stats. What is Free Throw Rate (FTr)? Free Throw Rate (FTr) calculates how often a player gets to the free throw line compared to field goal attempts. For example, if a player takes 5 free throws and 20 field goals, their FTr is 0.25. This NBA player stat reflects aggressiveness and efficiency, supporting NBA scoring stats. What is Points per Shot Attempt (PPS)? Points per Shot Attempt (PPS) measures how many points a player scores per shot taken, including free throws. For instance, if a player scores 25 points on 20 attempts, their PPS is 1.25. This NBA player stat highlights efficiency and complements NBA scoring stats."}
{"question": "What Are Daily NBA Projections?", "answer": "Daily NBA Projections estimate each player's statistical output for today's games using per-minute production rates from the current season. Each player's season averages for points, rebounds, assists, steals, blocks, turnovers, three-pointers, two-pointers, and free throws per minute are multiplied by their projected minutes. A pace factor adjusts for matchup tempo — calculated from both teams' pace relative to the league average and clamped to ±5%. Defensive boosts reflect how many more or fewer stats the opposing defense allows compared to league average, also clamped to ±5%. An analyst-set FPPM Boost multiplier captures lineup changes, injury news, and usage shifts. DraftKings fantasy points use: PTS + 0.5×3PM + 1.25×REB + 1.5×AST + 2×STL + 2×BLK − 0.5×TO. FanDuel fantasy points use: 3×3PM + 2×2PM + 1×FTM + 1.2×REB + 1.5×AST + 3×STL + 3×BLK − 1×TO. Projections are updated multiple times daily as injury reports and lineup news develop."}

